🛡️ Safeguarding Azure Infrastructure with Terraform
This guide explains the secure management of credentials using the Three-File Architecture: .env, variables.tf, and provider.tf.

1. Objective: The "Secret Separation" Principle
The primary objective is Separation of Concerns. By isolating secrets from the logic, we ensure that:

Confidentiality: API keys and Secrets never enter version control (GitHub).

Integrity: The core infrastructure code remains generic and reusable.

Safety: Reduces the risk of accidental "leaks" that could lead to unauthorized cloud access.

2. Why We Use These Files
📂 .env (The Secret Vault)
The .env file is a local-only shell script. Its purpose is to store sensitive values as environment variables. By using the TF_VAR_ prefix, Terraform can automatically ingest these values without them being hardcoded in your .tf files.

📂 variables.tf (The Interface)
This file acts as the "Input Definition" layer. It tells Terraform exactly which variables are required for the project to run. It allows for metadata tagging, such as description and the sensitive = true flag, which protects secrets from being printed in terminal logs.

📂 provider.tf (The Connection)
The provider file is the "Bridge" to Azure. It uses the variables defined in variables.tf to authenticate your session. Because it uses variables instead of hardcoded strings, this file can be safely shared and committed to Git.

3. Implementation & Code Syntax
📂 File 1: .env
Note: This file must be added to your .gitignore. Ensure there is an empty line above and below the code block below.


# .env - DO NOT COMMIT TO GIT

export TF_VAR_tenant_id="your-tenant-id"

export TF_VAR_subscription_id="your-sub-id"

export TF_VAR_client_id="your-client-id"

export TF_VAR_client_secret="your-client-secret"

📂 File 2: variables.tf


variable "tenant_id" {
  description = "The Tenant ID for the Azure Provider"
  type        = string
}

variable "subscription_id" {
  description = "The Subscription ID for the Azure Provider"
  type        = string
}

variable "client_id" {
  description = "The Client ID for the Azure Provider"
  type        = string
}

variable "client_secret" {
  description = "The Client Secret for the Azure Provider"
  type        = string
  sensitive   = true
}

📂 File 3: provider.tf

provider "azurerm" {
  features {}

  client_id       = var.client_id
  client_secret   = var.client_secret
  subscription_id = var.subscription_id
  tenant_id       = var.tenant_id
}

4. How to Connect the Files (The Workflow)
To link these files together in your terminal, follow these steps:

Initialize the Environment: Load your secrets into the current terminal session.

Bash

source .env
Verify the Mapping: Terraform looks for variables starting with TF_VAR_. It strips the prefix and matches the name to your variables.tf.

Execute Terraform:

Bash

terraform init
terraform plan

⚠️ Critical Security Step

Ensure your .gitignore file contains the following to prevent accidental leaks:

.env
*.tfstate
.terraform/





